OPEN(2)                        P32W Unix                         OPEN(2)

NAME
       open, creat - open or create a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <fcntl.h>

       int open(const char *path, int flags, ...);
       int creat(const char *path, mode_t mode);

DESCRIPTION
       open opens the file path and returns a file descriptor for
       it: the lowest one not in use. The file offset starts at 0.
       flags is one of

       O_RDONLY (0)   open for reading
       O_WRONLY (1)   open for writing
       O_RDWR   (2)   open for reading and writing

       or-ed with any of

       O_CREAT  (0100)
              If the file does not exist, create it as a regular
              file with the mode given as a third argument, less
              the bits set in the file creation mask (umask(2)).
              Its owner and group are the caller's effective IDs.
              The directory must be writable. If it does exist, it
              is opened as without O_CREAT, unless it is a
              directory (EEXIST).
       O_EXCL   (0200)
              With O_CREAT: fail with EEXIST if the file exists.
       O_TRUNC  (01000)
              Truncate a regular file to length 0 (other files are
              left alone).
       O_APPEND (02000)
              Before each write, move the offset to the end of the
              file.

       Reading needs read permission and writing needs write
       permission on the file. A directory may only be opened for
       reading. Opening a device special file calls its driver: see
       the devices below.

       creat(path, mode) is open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT |
       O_TRUNC, mode).

       The descriptor is inherited by fork(2) and kept across
       exec(2). The first terminal a process opens when it has no
       controlling terminal becomes its controlling terminal.

       DEVICES
       /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1   the terminals; see ioctl(2)
       /dev/tty               the caller's controlling terminal
       /dev/null              reads give end of file; writes vanish
       /dev/port              the I/O card's 8-bit input and
                              output ports
       /dev/mem               (root only) not implemented
       /dev/disk              the CompactFlash filesystem area,
                              block device 0 (through the buffer
                              cache)
       /dev/rdisk             (root only) the whole card, raw
       /dev/ram               a 256 KiB RAM disk, block device 1

RETURN VALUE
       A file descriptor, 0 to 19; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  The file does not exist and O_CREAT is not given,
               or a directory in path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path; or the file may not be read or written as
               asked; or the file must be created and its directory
               is not writable; or it is /dev/rdisk or /dev/mem and
               the caller is not the super-user.
       EEXIST  O_CREAT and O_EXCL were given and the file exists;
               or O_CREAT was given and path is a directory.
       EISDIR  A directory was opened for writing.
       ENOSPC  No free inode for a new file, or no room in the
               directory.
       ENXIO   A device special file names a device that does not
               exist (a bad major or minor number), or /dev/tty was
               opened by a process without a controlling terminal.
       ENFILE  The system's open-file table is full (128).
       EMFILE  The process has 20 files open.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       close(2), read(2), write(2), lseek(2), dup(2), umask(2),
       stat(2), ioctl(2), stdio(3)

BUGS
       Creating a file with a mode that lacks write permission for
       its owner (say creat(name, 0444)) fails with EACCES for an
       ordinary user, although the file is created; V7 opened it.
       O_TRUNC is applied before the descriptor is allocated, so a
       full file table (ENFILE, EMFILE) still truncates the file.
       There is no O_NONBLOCK, O_NDELAY or O_NOCTTY, and no fcntl.
       The mode argument is fetched even without O_CREAT.
